id: pm-kxci8x
title: "Single source of surface tiering: one contract-declared tier per command drives CLI help, MCP tool profiles, completions, and docs together"
description: "Four separate efforts each decide, independently, which part of pm's surface an agent sees first: tiered CLI help, MCP tool profiles capping the exposed tool count, hiding completion helper entrypoints, and hiding the internal test-runs worker. Each is correct on its own and all four answer the same question — what tier is this command in — with four mechanisms that will drift apart the moment one is edited without the others. Declare the tier once, as a field on the command contract, and derive all four surfaces from it. The tiers themselves are the decision: a core tier an agent must know to work, a standard tier it reaches for by name, a full tier that exists and is documented, and an internal tier that is reachable but never advertised. Deriving everything from one field also makes the one-screen claim testable rather than aspirational, because the core tier's rendered size is a number the gate can check."
type: Task
status: closed
priority: 2
tags[6]: agent-ux,"area:cli","area:mcp",contracts,discoverability,simplification
created_at: "2026-07-25T12:28:50.389Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-23T10:18:02.793Z"
closed_at: "2026-08-23T10:18:02.087Z"
completed_at: "2026-08-23T10:18:02.087Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 360
acceptance_criteria: "Every command declares exactly one tier in contract data, and help, MCP tool profiles, shell completions, and generated docs derive visibility from that field alone; Every command declares exactly one noun (capability family) in the same contract record as its tier, and every declared noun has at least one command, checked in both directions against the command registration rather than against a list kept beside it; A test asserts no surface maintains its own independent visibility list; Agent-facing routing surfaces - skills, guide topics, and generated docs - derive their capability map from the declared noun field rather than hand-authoring one; The core tier's rendered help size is asserted against the one-screen budget, making the claim mechanically checked; The first landing changes no visible surface, proven by before-and-after snapshots of help, tool lists, and completions; Changing a command's tier is a single-field change whose effect on all four surfaces appears in one regenerated snapshot diff; Package-contributed commands declare a tier and a noun through the same fields, so extensions cannot bypass tiering or routing"
goal: project management = context management
objective: "Surface visibility is one declared property, not four independently maintained lists"
value: Four mechanisms answering one question is a drift generator; drift here means an agent sees different surfaces through CLI and MCP and cannot trust either
impact: "Consolidates four visibility mechanisms into one contract field; the visible surfaces should be unchanged on the first landing, proven by snapshots"
outcome: "Raising or lowering a command's visibility is a one-field change that updates help, MCP tools, completions, and docs at once"
parent: pm-n7rr
risk: low
confidence: 75
resolution: "Completed by making visibility tier and capability family contract fields the shared source for CLI, MCP, completion, guide, generated-doc, and extension projections, with rendered help budgeting and regression gates."
expected_result: "Every command and extension command declares one validated tier and capability family, and CLI help, MCP profiles, completions, guides, and generated docs derive their routing from those contracts within the enforced root-help budget."
actual_result: "Contract-derived tier and family projections are active across CLI, MCP, completions, guides, docs, and extension packages; built-in metadata remains isolated, dynamic root help is budgeted from its rendered payload, and all focused, static, lint, compatibility, grammar, and exact-coverage gates pass."
